Link to this headingBonus: bouncing ball playground
In the very first demo on this blog post, I shared a bouncing ball. Here’s a playground with detailed comments showing how this effect works:
Code Playground
<style>
  /*
    This keyframe causes an item to bounce into the air. It pauses briefly
    on the ground (5% of the total duration) so that the squash effect
    doesn’t happen while the element is in the air.

    (If this still doesn’t make sense: picture bouncing a very soft ball. It
    will splat on the ground for a sec before bouncing back up.)
  */
  @keyframes bounce {
    0% {
      /*
        I’m using the “translate” shorthand here, rather than “transform:
        translate();”, since we’re going to use two different transform
        functions on the same element. The next keyframe uses “scale()”.
      */
      translate: 0 0;
    }
    5% {
      translate: 0 0;
    }
    100% {
      translate: 0 -100px;
    }
  }

  /*
    This keyframe handles the squashing and stretching. It begins with the
    element splatting on the ground, bulging out horizontally. Then, very
    quickly, it stretches out vertically, before eventually reverting to
    its natural size.
  */
  @keyframes squash {
    0% {
      scale:
        var(--squash-ratio)
        calc(1 / var(--stretch-ratio));
    }
    10% {
      scale:
        calc(1 / var(--squash-ratio))
        var(--stretch-ratio);
    }
    100% {
      scale: 1 1;
    }
  }
  .ball {
    /*
      It’s actually *really* tough, trying to model gravity using cubic
      Bézier curves. This curve is the closest I’ve been able to get, though
      I’m still not 100% happy with it:
    */
    --curve: cubic-bezier(0.123, 0.5, 0.5, 1);

    /*
      Tweak these values to change how intense the effect is.
      Valid values between 1 and 2:
    */
    --stretch-ratio: 1.25;
    --squash-ratio: 1.5;

    width: 50px;
    height: 50px;
    background: hsl(45deg 100% 50%);
    border-radius: 50%;
    /*
      Finally, we apply our two keyframe animations!
      For “bounce”, we’ve only modeled the bounce upwards, but we can include
      the fall downwards with the “alternate” keyword, causing the keyframe
      to play in reverse when it ends. This also means that two “bounce”
      iterations happen for every “squash” iteration, which is why the
      duration is 1/2 as long:
    */
    animation:
      squash 800ms linear infinite,
      bounce 400ms var(--curve) infinite alternate;

    /*
      IMPORTANT: we want the scale origin to be along the bottom edge, so
      that when it gets squashed, it stays glued to the ground:
    */
    transform-origin: center bottom;
  }
</style>
<div class="wrapper">
  <div class="ball"></div>
</div>
Like I said above, I think that there are way more practical ways to use this effect than this 😅. But, if you do find yourself needing a bounce animation, I hope this playground helps!
And if you do wind up squashing and/or stretching something in your work, I’d love to see it! You can share it with me on Bluesky(opens in new tab) or by email.
